rtain people who do certain  tasks in the in the order of making  music  it's been always very linear and i think  i had to break the line and just say no  i like  i like being involved in this part and i  like giving it away and then i like  being involved in this part  and just learning where where my  strengths play into  what i love to do um it's not that i  have to do all of one thing or  all of the the other thing i enjoy the  collaboration and i enjoy  um sort of the tag team effort of it  so that was one thing that i i learned  from being out there and working with  people out there  and also just working on my own music  you know  i wasn't looking at a wall um the same  wall  working on on production anymore i had a  window with a  view of the mountains and i just felt  like i was in this super scenic like  retreat  so it sort of felt like i was on  vacation making the album  which which undoes is making like a  kenny chesney  like beach chair kind of an album now  yeah not necessarily but and it also  allowed me to fall in love with  you know there's a lot of parts of los  angeles that have a very historic  place in music making i can't tell you  how many times i've watched the you know  sound city documentary and things like  that  um also allowed me to go back to  i really like old music  i say old music yeah i mean you know  whatever  i like older music i love stuff that was  made in certain era it's because  those eras were full of instrumentation  that is timeless to me that  doesn't ever get old we saw it this year  one of my favorite albums of all time  got another swing at life this year  because somebody posted a tick tock on a  freaking skateboard singing you know a  fleetwood back song  and then all of a sudden everybody was  paying attention to my favorite  fleetwood mac one of my  top three all-time favorite albums was  was on like a spotify playlist like  freaking like whatever it was it was on  this like super popular playlist that i  listened to all the time  but here was this super dope old sound  that actually still  sounds new and fresh so i think i just  reconnected with that too like i  want to make music that sounds timeless  and doesn't just  follow trends or just study what's  working and apply that  um i think you already have done that i  feel like i've talked to a lot of  artists and i find it to be such a  redeeming quality within an artist  whether it be  like i talked to moby um mclemore  like some of these guys where you can't  like you it's hard to even put your  finger on exactly what the genre is  because you're creating your own which  to me  says it's really you're really accessing  it from  spirit or from your um your truth  or who you are and that is not something  that's quantifiable that's not  boxable it just is what it is and so i i  i  i admire you for sticking with that and  in fact even like  going in deeper have there been aspects  of the going in deeper that have felt  really uncomfortable yeah for sure  i mean you have to uh honestly last  night one of  one of my friends had a brilliant quote  i don't know if you heard this but  he said you know if you if you want to  have massive success you also have to  have a massive trash can  it's a ridiculous statement but it's so  true like you have to be  my my ratio is like 100 songs to get 10  that consistently it doesn't matter  like i it doesn't matter that i'm  learning how to make  better music or learning how to access  that thing quicker or more efficiently  or be more present  i'm still going to need to write like  three songs to get the point across and  get to the place where and sometimes  it's the first song sometimes you end up  tossing everything that happens after  because you think you can do it better  and then you realize you already did it  more you know  honest um so i think that  you know there's scary stuff it's been  um  trying things and  you know knowing full well  that i mean anything outside of the i